WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.456
Central Ideas and Details

00:00:02.836 --> 00:00:07.127
Subject plus claim. Not too narrow, broad or extreme.

00:00:08.327 --> 00:00:09.325
How to spot it

00:00:09.725 --> 00:00:12.133
The question says “main idea of the text”

00:00:12.683 --> 00:00:15.142
A short passage, sometimes a story

00:00:15.692 --> 00:00:18.529
It’s testing: the author’s central point

00:00:19.729 --> 00:00:20.524
The method

00:00:20.924 --> 00:00:23.117
Step 1: Find the subject

00:00:23.397 --> 00:00:24.884
Who or what is this about?

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:27.416
Step 2: Find the claim

00:00:27.696 --> 00:00:30.625
What’s said about it? Watch “but” and “however”.

00:00:31.175 --> 00:00:33.936
Step 3: Predict in one sentence

00:00:34.216 --> 00:00:35.557
Subject + claim.

00:00:36.107 --> 00:00:38.366
Step 4: Filter the choices

00:00:38.646 --> 00:00:41.592
Cut too narrow, too broad, too extreme.

00:00:42.142 --> 00:00:43.252
In a few words

00:00:43.402 --> 00:00:45.726
Subject. Claim. Filter.

00:00:46.926 --> 00:00:48.066
Answer filters

00:00:48.466 --> 00:00:49.223
Too narrow

00:00:49.503 --> 00:00:51.840
One true detail, not the whole point.

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:53.210
Too broad

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.411
Bigger than what the passage covers.

00:00:55.961 --> 00:00:56.931
Too extreme

00:00:57.211 --> 00:01:00.976
“Proves”, “always”, “never” on a careful passage.

00:01:01.526 --> 00:01:02.484
After the turn

00:01:02.764 --> 00:01:05.474
The point usually follows “but” or “however”.

00:01:06.674 --> 00:01:07.714
Let’s try one

00:01:08.114 --> 00:01:13.876
For much of the twentieth century, scientists assumed that plants were passive organisms that simply

00:01:13.876 --> 00:01:16.796
responded to sunlight and water.

00:01:16.796 --> 00:01:23.776
Recent studies, however, show that plants can detect chemical signals released by damaged neighbors

00:01:23.776 --> 00:01:27.776
and respond by producing defensive compounds of their own.

00:01:27.776 --> 00:01:33.726
Some species even appear to adjust their growth depending on whether nearby plants are relatives.

00:01:34.276 --> 00:01:36.992
Which choice best states the main idea of the text?

00:01:37.642 --> 00:01:42.102
First, the old view: scientists assumed plants were passive.

00:01:42.652 --> 00:01:49.095
Then the turn, however: plants can detect signals and respond. That’s the main idea.

00:01:49.645 --> 00:01:57.528
Too narrow: C, just one detail. Too extreme: D. Not the point: A. The answer is B.

00:01:58.728 --> 00:01:59.724
Common traps

00:02:00.124 --> 00:02:01.196
The true detail

00:02:01.476 --> 00:02:03.407
Accurate, but only one piece.

00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:04.964
Word matching

00:02:05.244 --> 00:02:07.181
Same words, different meaning.

00:02:08.381 --> 00:02:10.237
Central Ideas and Details

00:02:10.517 --> 00:02:12.842
Subject. Claim. Filter.

00:02:13.392 --> 00:02:14.165
Spot it

00:02:14.365 --> 00:02:15.798
“main idea of the text”

00:02:16.348 --> 00:02:17.056
Steps

00:02:17.256 --> 00:02:18.919
1. Find the subject

00:02:19.199 --> 00:02:21.940
2. Find the claim (watch but / however)

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:25.206
3. Predict: subject + claim

00:02:25.486 --> 00:02:28.353
4. Cut too narrow / broad / extreme

00:02:28.903 --> 00:02:29.656
Remember

00:02:29.856 --> 00:02:31.535
Too narrow = one detail

00:02:31.815 --> 00:02:34.095
Too broad = more than the text covers

00:02:34.375 --> 00:02:37.418
Too extreme = proves, always, never

00:02:37.698 --> 00:02:39.799
The point often comes after the turn

00:02:40.349 --> 00:02:40.954
Trap

00:02:41.154 --> 00:02:43.316
A true detail that’s only one piece

